+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more number and unit groups using `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes),
+and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may be used at most once and in that order, without
+spaces. Units are case-insensitive. Negative numbers and decimals are not accepted. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` value,
+for example as a default, is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
